"1. Use LensKit 0.14.4 APIs throughout the experiment and ensure all three recommenders are implemented with that version: ALS, ItemKNN, and Pop.\n2. Load the raw files from the working directory using the exact filenames: u.data, VideoGames.csv, and user_taggedartists-timestamps.dat.\n3. Parse each dataset into a unified interaction table with at least user, item, and timestamp columns when available.\n4. Apply iterative 5-core filtering separately to each dataset so every remaining user and item has at least 5 interactions.\n5. For MovieLens100K and Amazon Video Games, convert explicit ratings to implicit interactions by keeping only records with rating > 3 and discarding lower ratings before 5-core filtering.\n6. Treat Last.FM tag events as implicit interactions; deduplicate repeated user-item interactions so evaluation uses binary relevance.\n7. Generate exactly 5 distinct random seeds and use them only to control the train/test splitting procedure.\n8. For each dataset and seed, perform a user-based 80/20 holdout split with randomness reproducible from the seed and no leakage between train and test interactions.\n9. Ensure every test user has at least 1 held-out interaction after splitting; handle edge cases deterministically.\n10. Train each algorithm separately on each dataset-seed training split using standard/default hyperparameters available in LensKit 0.14.4.\n11. Produce top-N recommendations for all evaluable test users while excluding items already seen in that user\u2019s training history.\n12. Evaluate recommendation quality with nDCG@1, nDCG@5, nDCG@10 and Precision@1, Precision@5, Precision@10 for every algorithm-dataset-seed run.\n13. Aggregate metrics at the user level into per-run summary scores, then compile a results table indexed by dataset, algorithm, and split seed.\n14. Quantify seed sensitivity by summarizing metric variation across the 5 seeds for each dataset-algorithm pair, including mean and standard deviation.\n15. Conduct a short statistical analysis across seeds, at minimum reporting whether seed-induced differences are practically meaningful and comparing variability across algorithms and datasets; include confidence intervals or repeated-measures significance tests where appropriate to the 5-seed design.\n16. Save all intermediate and final results in machine-readable files (e.g., CSV) so the experiment is reproducible and auditable.\n17. Set global random states where relevant and log package versions, chosen seeds, dataset sizes after preprocessing, and number of train/test interactions per split.\n18. Include validation checks that confirm 5-core constraints, successful implicit conversion, non-empty train/test sets, and successful metric computation for every run.\n19. Structure the code to run the full Cartesian product of 3 datasets \u00d7 3 algorithms \u00d7 5 seeds automatically without manual intervention.\n20. Produce a concise final report or printed summary highlighting average accuracy and seed-driven variability for each dataset and algorithm."